  "answer_text": "## What the verifier is complaining about\n\n`bpf_local_irq_save()` enters an **IRQ-disabled region**. Inside that region the program is not allowed to do anything that could sleep.\n\n`helper_a()` is a **global** (non-`static`) `__noinline` function, so the verifier treats it as an independent subprogram and verifies it in isolation. Because its body can reach a sleepable helper (`bpf_copy_from_user()`), the verifier marks the whole subprogram as \"sleepable\". Calling a sleepable global function while IRQs are disabled is therefore rejected:\n\n```\nsleepable global function helper_a() called in IRQ-disabled region\n```\n\nThe call at line 499 sits between `bpf_local_irq_save()` (line 498) and `bpf_local_irq_restore()` (line 500), i.e. inside the critical section (depth 1), which is exactly what triggers the rejection.\n\nNote that just making `helper_a()` `static` would not help: the verifier would then inline-verify its body in the IRQ-disabled context, reach `bpf_copy_from_user()` (which is itself sleepable), and reject that call for the same reason. The real problem is that a potentially-sleeping call happens inside the IRQ-disabled region.\n\n## Smallest fix\n\nMove the `helper_a()` call outside the IRQ-disabled critical section (exactly what the verifier suggests). In a `syscall` (sleepable) program that call is perfectly fine once IRQs are re-enabled.\n\n```c\nSEC(\"?syscall\")\nint entry(void *ctx)\n{\n\tunsigned long flags;\n\n\tbpf_local_irq_save(&flags);\n\tbpf_local_irq_restore(&flags);\n\thelper_a(0);\n\treturn 0;\n}\n```\n\n(or equivalently place `helper_a(0);` before `bpf_local_irq_save()`).\n\nThis keeps the possibly-sleeping call out of the IRQ-disabled window, so the \"operation may sleep\" / \"sleepable global function in IRQ-disabled region\" check no longer fires, and the program verifies.",
